#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093082} :activity/id #uuid "eaf91217-4388-5eb6-8d99-ece4485ba63a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049612 :topic/id #uuid "04817f30-147e-5cb9-b0bb-c54b2e323640" :topic/math-academy-id 1000} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093357 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the area of the region bounded by the polar curve $r = 3\\cos\\theta$ and the rays $\\theta_1 = 0$ and $\\theta_2 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ as shown in blue in the picture below. Can we calculate this area using polar coordinates?\n\n![](images/2c/2c7e638a338aed2155ef5bf388be1f83a3a67d2296d5be09987b099a07edacbd.png)\n\nIn general, the area of the region bounded by a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ and the rays $\\theta=\\theta_1$ and $\\theta=\\theta_2$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2 \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the half-circle above can be obtained as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "37d5627c-6e4c-5ffe-b3f4-9efac9c1cc1c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1126 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "124bbd3f-149a-58b4-a192-cddec4a55c87" :step/math-academy-id 636917 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093083}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092237 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092089 :question/id #uuid "0d1dcc59-e7af-5a86-87ba-aa7ee1b23767" :question/math-academy-id "e-632"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "974a88f6-ff6a-5183-aeaf-6019e09061ec" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049303}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529149}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded by a Petal of a Polar Rose"} :step/id #uuid "3976f903-173b-5b28-82c6-c4d5e43d84ee" :step/math-academy-id 636918 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093084}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093084 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092238 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092090 :question/id #uuid "6040d180-ed1d-52b8-809c-b5820352d3d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-633"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98c9f0f1-be39-5893-b757-7a4750b07ab5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529179}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded by the Loop of a Limaçon"} :step/id #uuid "4a4e1d0d-4d58-52c3-9254-7fbdf1cb4931" :step/math-academy-id 636921 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093127}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093127 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092268 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092105 :question/id #uuid "a2d8dca5-a0f3-5b6f-81f5-4eea46ee70d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-6822"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a62e8412-1733-577c-bd60-25395e6fa908" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529177}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Areas Bounded by Other Polar Curves"} :step/id #uuid "545d497e-388a-508b-8784-5ae8f6439681" :step/math-academy-id 636924 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093145}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093145 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093452 :tutorial/content "We've been using the following formula for the area of the region bounded by a polar curve ![](images/70/7031a0f3ab71df36bc36e925fcf4f04dbfc4feec5f374092855ef8d97b104452.svg) and the rays ![](images/38/38b971de9610efe2ed0de9f10956c73cf5cd967a86a5bcbb076791f369c22bef.svg) and ![](images/81/818e2605a585628c4becd20664b58007dbd2348ca664c80be6a838868a64693a.svg)\n\n![](images/55/55ffcf934c3a9e33a3376fa1796e6fb68ad1198d719db5ea094fe8eb46c5f47d.svg)\n\nBut where does this formula come from?\n\nTo see where the formula comes from, we can think about breaking up the area bounded by a polar curve into infinitesimally small sectors of circles.\n\nAs shown in the graph below, each sector has a radius of ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) and spans an infinitesimally small angle, ![](images/09/0904a69fa5e6a3a10eaf74b3fdd865db92761eeeffffae1c0badd33ffdf5b34b.svg)\n\n![](images/82/82be86e7092a97ca5b2f1423f32b15030af0c8c63bf47ece8be40b45720ac905.png)\n\nThe ratio of the area of a sector to the full area of the circle must be the same as the ratio of the angle of the sector to a full revolution of the circle. So, if the area of a sector is ![](images/0b/0b2376d17b173024d6f6c82faaff585293e9830ba7ce0764ecf8338adcd14994.svg) then we can set up and solve the following proportion:\n\n![](images/5d/5d318385ab16ee14d784b33c00180a44ade7cee2e97ffeafd290171d0d44868f.svg)\n\nFinally, to find the area under the curve, we can integrate the areas of all the sectors:\n\n![](images/a7/a75940f74a02ceadff519af028783a0e704fba8a73c4abf36c6a371deb56dee7.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "1ba36924-1af3-515d-ac60-aab46240a7e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4582 :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind the Formula"} :step/id #uuid "a348433a-cb9d-5ca7-a360-627ea0f7f338" :step/math-academy-id 636927}] :activity/title "Finding the Area of a Polar Region" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092396}]}
